# The existence form of Čebotarev implies the Frobenius input, at an arbitrary universe

The final step of the universe-polymorphic Čebotarev chapter: the existence form
`NK.UniverseWeakNU.ChebotarevInfiniteU` (every `σ : Gal(N/K)` is a Frobenius element at
infinitely many primes of `K`, with the base field `K` in the bottom universe) discharges the
arithmetic input `NK.UniverseWeakNU.ChebotarevFrobeniusInputU` consumed by the weak
Neukirch–Uchida chain.

This is the universe-polymorphic copy of the universe-0 step
`NK.ChebotarevGeneral.chebotarevFrobeniusInput_of_infinite` of
`Submission/Work/ChebotarevGeneral/RatLevel.lean`; the only thing that changes is that the top
field `N` lives in `Type u`, and the resulting definition is *not* definitionally equal to the
universe-0 one, because the countable set of primes `T` is a `Finset ℕ` in both cases but the
field `N` it refers to is not.

## The argument

`ChebotarevFrobeniusInputU` asks, for a finite Galois extension `N/ℚ` with `N : Type u`, for an
element `σ : Gal(N/ℚ)` and a finite set `T` of rational primes, for a prime `p ∉ T` which is
unramified in `N` and above which a Frobenius element for `σ` exists.

`ChebotarevInfiniteU` applied at the base field `ℚ` (bottom universe) and the top field `N`
gives that the Frobenius set of `σ` in `N/ℚ` is infinite; discarding the finitely many ramified
primes (they are finite by `finite_setOf_not_isUnramified`) leaves an infinite set, and mapping
it to the rational primes by `ratPrimeOf` — injective — leaves an infinite set of rational
primes, which therefore cannot be contained in the finite set `T`.
-/

